      "id": "part_03-ch_15",
      "position": 18,
      "kind": "chapter",
      "prefix": "Chapter 15",
      "title": "Chaharshanbe Suri: The Fire Festival before Spring",
      "summary": "On the eve of the last Wednesday of the solar year, streets across Iran and the diaspora roar with crackling bonfires as communities leap through fire to cast off winter.",
      "content": "![Vibrant nocturnal celebration with leaping figures silhouetted against glowing embers](https://assets.lingocafe.app/traditions-01/images/chaharshanbe-suri-persian-fire-festival-hero.webp)\n\nCelebrated on the eve of the final Wednesday before the vernal equinox, Chaharshanbe Suri acts as the fiery overture to Nowruz. In ancient Zoroastrian traditions, the final days of the year were dedicated to the *Faravahar*—the ancestral spirits who visited the earthly realm to witness the seasonal renewal. Bonfires were kindled upon rooftops and hills to illuminate their path and cleanse the physical world of demonic impurities that gathered during the winter cold.\n\nIn modern Iran and across the global diaspora, the festival maintains its thrilling, populist vitality. As evening descends, communities build odd-numbered sequences of bonfires along avenues and alleyways. Participants sprint and leap over the flames, reciting the ancient formula: *Zardi-ye man az to, sorkhi-ye to az man* ('My yellow paleness to you, your red vitality to me'). Through this symbolic combustion, participants divest themselves of psychological fatigue, melancholy, and physical ailments.\n\n![Artisanal bowl of seven dried fruits, roasted pistachios, and saffron almonds](https://assets.lingocafe.app/traditions-01/images/chaharshanbe-suri-persian-fire-festival-detail.webp)\n\nThe evening is enriched by distinctive folk customs such as *Ghashoq-zani* (spoon-banging), where cloaked youths knock spoons against copper bowls at neighborly thresholds to receive treats, mimicking ancestral spirits seeking hospitality. Families consume *ajil-e moshkel-gosha*—a consecrated blend of seven roasted nuts, dried mulberries, and raisins believed to resolve personal difficulties. Chaharshanbe Suri represents a triumph of communal solidarity, transforming the street into a vibrant sanctuary of light before the dawn of the new year."
